AUDIT RECEIPT: ARMv7-A T32 D000 THROUGH DFFF FINITE DECODE.

Date of source work: 2026-10-11.
Work origin: https://github.com/isomorphisms/flexible-pipes/issues/91
Source PR: https://github.com/dilapidated-shed/English-major-C/pull/3
Status: inspected and committed source plus transient diagnostic checks.
NOT English Major C compiler/interpreter acceptance.
NOT full ARMv7-A ISA qualification.
NOT a physical ARM execution report.
NOT independent AICI acceptance.

I. SPECIFICATION THAT WAS CHECKED.

Primary architecture:
ARM DDI 0406C.d, publication ID040418,
A6.2.6 Table A6-8, A8.8.18 T1, UDF T1, SVC T1.

Committed specification:
architecture/armv7-a/t32/
Every conditional-branch and supervisor-call halfword.english

Committed full encoding inventory:
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-and-exception-halfwords.tsv

Committed all-four-flag predicate table:
fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-flag-truth-table.tsv

Source-derived trace:
examples/A conditional branch chooses which exceptional instruction is next.english

The declaration "every Dxxx halfword" is scoped to exactly
hexadecimal D000 through DFFF. It includes 4096 distinct
16-bit instruction halfwords. It DOES NOT cover other
Thumb 16-bit halfwords or the two-halfword combinations
used to represent 32-bit Thumb instructions.

II. FOREIGN DIAGNOSTIC COMPILER AND DECODER.

The diagnostic host was a Linux x86-64 container.
Observed clang version: 17.0.0, built from LLVM project revision
10999b6d034fe318f3d56c83bddb6572593a8bb0.
Observed llvm-objdump version: LLVM 17.0.0.
The exact tool executable file digests were NOT recorded.
The diagnostic selected target:
  clang --target=armv7a-none-eabi -mthumb
  llvm-objdump --triple=thumbv7-none-eabi -d

The foreign diagnostic generated a temporary assembler source with:
    .syntax unified
    .arch armv7-a
    .thumb
    .text
    .global all_d
    .thumb_func
    all_d:
and a sequence of .hword directives listing every unsigned halfword
from 0xD000 through 0xDFFF in strictly ascending numerical order.
Sixteen halfwords were written per .hword directive line.

The ephemeral assembler input contained no first-party maintained
application program, no English Major C frontend and no ICK output.
The assembler produced an ELF32 ARM object.
The decoder produced an ordinary sequential disassembly of
the object's .text bytes; it did not execute the branches.

SHA256 of the ephemeral diagnostic assembly source:
  307d73b3687c5e91d173e0206178738e4e51dad64aebe940f682aeef837e7de8
SHA256 of the diagnostic ELF32 object:
  8414f37af17957dadf957b013b6617898df79cfa62cbdbb0a22335543b1e1650
SHA256 of the decoder's text output:
  3abc6984c04a724c43d2ab20fac1e9fb1c4158beb0e179e7108bf0a278a69642

These digests bind the specific transient observed artifacts.
The artifacts themselves were NOT committed to this repository,
so their SHA256 values are not a source-code reproducibility
guarantee and are not evidence of a future regenerated result.

III. WHAT THE INDEPENDENT CHECK OBSERVED.

The complete disassembly contained 4096 decoded instruction
halfwords in the expected increasing order. Each printed raw
halfword matched hexadecimal D000+(instruction-byte-offset/2).

All fourteen condition-code classes were present:
  EQ, NE, CS/HS, CC/LO, MI, PL, VS, VC,
  HI, LS, GE, LT, GT, LE.

Each of those fourteen groups contained exactly 256
different immediate bytes. The total was 3584 T1
conditional branch encodings.

For EVERY one of those 3584 conditional branches,
the diagnostic printed signed byte displacement was
checked against the independently calculated rule:
    signed_imm8 = imm8, if imm8 < 128;
                  imm8 - 256, otherwise;
    signed_displacement_in_bytes = 2*signed_imm8.
No discrepancy was observed.

The group 0xDE00 through 0xDEFF contained exactly
256 UDF-class encodings. The group 0xDF00 through
0xDFFF contained exactly 256 SVC-class encodings.

The first attempt to compare mnemonic names LITERALLY
failed. Although ARM Table A6-8 classifies all 256
DE encodings as UDF, LLVM 17 prints:
  0xDEF9 as "__brkdiv0" rather than "udf";
  0xDEFE as "trap" rather than "udf".
The first attempt counted 254 literal "udf" entries,
one "__brkdiv0", and one "trap"; its original regular
expression also missed the underscore-prefixed entry.

The check was repaired by keeping the real encoded
halfword and ARM normative UDF classification fixed,
while recognizing the two foreign mnemonic spellings
as decoder aliases. After that explicit normalization:
  4096 decoded halfwords matched in numerical order;
  3584 branch displacements matched;
  3584 condition branches grouped correctly;
  256 UDF-class encodings matched;
  256 SVC-class encodings matched;
  zero remaining differences were observed.

No source-language meaning was changed merely to make
LLVM print a preferred word. No physical processor ran
any of the decoded instructions.

IV. REPOSITORY-LEVEL DATA INTEGRITY CHECK.

The stored TSV table was fetched back through GitHub
from the actual draft source branch. Each of its
4096 data rows was separately re-evaluated using:
  first high nibble must be D;
  next high nibble 0..D => conditional B T1;
  next high nibble E => UDF T1;
  next high nibble F => SVC T1;
  low byte is unsigned 0..255;
  conditional B displacement is the signed low byte
  multiplied by two;
  other two classes have no branch displacement;
  B T1 has English grant operation 43 OUTSIDE IT;
  UDF T1 and SVC T1 have no permission in this profile.
The row count and every field matched the expected
class and formula. This is a data readback/format and
semantic arithmetic check, not native device execution.

V. ALL POSSIBLE ARCHITECTURAL FOUR-FLAG COMBINATIONS.

A second committed table describes every combination
of the four predicate flags N, Z, C and V. Each is
either zero or one, giving exactly sixteen bit patterns.

The table provides fourteen branches per pattern,
giving 16*14=224 independent rows.
For each row the chosen predicate, whether the branch
is taken, and the successor for an example instruction
at 0x1000 with zero displacement are stated.
Every one of the 224 rows was independently checked
against the specified Boolean condition formulas.
No discrepancy was observed.

A separate transient arithmetic oracle formed a
32-bit subtraction and its N, Z, C, V flags for every
ordered pair from this 13-value set:
  0x00000000, 0x00000001, 0x00000002, 0x00000003,
  0x7FFFFFFE, 0x7FFFFFFF,
  0x80000000, 0x80000001,
  0xFFFFFFFD, 0xFFFFFFFE, 0xFFFFFFFF,
  0x55555555, 0xAAAAAAAA.

There were 169 ordered pairs. Fourteen condition-code
predicates for each made 2366 results.
Those results were compared with independent unsigned
and signed integer relational or arithmetic-overflow
oracles where applicable. All 2366 checked results agreed.
These are limited arithmetic checks for selected inputs,
not exhaustive proof over all 2^64 ordered input pairs,
not actual ARM register execution and not an AICI verdict.

VI. SMALL BRANCH AND EXCEPTION PROVENANCE.

A separate transient diagnostic assembled the instruction
halfwords:
    0x2000, 0xD100, 0xD000, 0xDEFE,
    0x2103, 0xD000, 0xDF01

The expected decode classes in order are MOVS, BNE,
BEQ, UDF, MOVS, BEQ, SVC.
The foreign disassembler printed "trap" for DEFE.
This alias does not change ARM's architectural
classification or confer a permitted English operation.

SHA256 of that ephemeral source text:
  2aba930caece53d992f6359efb61e4b58b835db40e5ed5b8049caf3a382e29da
SHA256 of that diagnostic object:
  a1af4d48efb121b6ff968d008f684841fd29036aea4a637228ee26d58434ac29

A small independent temporary state model was evaluated
with these exact halfwords, narrow MOVS flag effects,
the branch condition predicates and T1 branch displacement
rules. Starting at offset zero, it produced:
  0x0000, 0x0002, 0x0004, 0x0008,
  0x000A, 0x000C;
  stop: ungranted SVC at 0x000C;
  R0=0, R1=3, N=0, Z=0, C/V unknown.

Starting at offset 0x0002 with entering Z=0, it produced:
  0x0002, 0x0006;
  stop: ungranted UDF at 0x0006.
No registers were invented for this second path.

This is a restricted independent arithmetic trace
calculation, not an executed ARM machine-state trace
and not an English Major C interpreter.

VII. DEFECTS AND ASSUMPTIONS NOT TO HIDE.

1. A decoder mnemonic is not always the canonical
   architectural instruction name: the UDF aliases
   in LLVM 17 are retained as observed evidence.
2. Full enumeration of a decode interval establishes
   classification of that interval only, not the
   entire ARM ISA and not all exceptional effects.
3. A conditional B T1 is UNPREDICTABLE inside IT.
   Merely invoking the generic rule that branches
   must be the last instruction in an IT block
   is not sufficient for this encoding.
4. Native SVC may be issued by unprivileged user code
   to request a supervisor exception, but its
   OS-specific effect is outside this grant.
5. UDF does not behave like a successful no-op.
6. A zero-specified branch displacement is not a
   branch to the instruction's A+2 sequential successor.
7. A tool run on x86-64 does not show that the
   target ARM machine executed the code.
8. The diagnostic tools are LLVM-family programs,
   so using clang and llvm-objdump is not an
   independent cross-vendor toolchain qualification.
   Their decoder agrees with ARM's published
   classification for this finite region, but this
   check does not formally prove the ARM pseudocode.

VIII. PROMOTION BLOCKERS.

A full ARMv7-A T32 claim remains blocked on all remaining
decode leaves, each instruction's exceptional effects,
ITSTATE validity, ARM architecture feature/privilege
profiles, optional SIMD/VFP and other extension
coverage, an actual machine/OS target declaration,
a working English Major C source processor, independent
behavioral fixtures with hostile variants, a qualified
ICK/NDK pipeline where maintained builds are attempted,
and independent AICI acceptance tied to the exact head.

This file must never be summarized as a complete
source-language implementation or a full CPU acceptance
merely because a transient decoder agreed on 4096 rows.

END OF OBSERVED DIAGNOSTIC RECEIPT.
